PMID- 42229162
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20260612
LR  - 20260612
IS  - 1873-4499 (Electronic)
IS  - 0899-7071 (Linking)
VI  - 136
DP  - 2026 Aug
TI  - The art of semantics in radiology reporting.
PG  - 110856
LID - S0899-7071(26)00148-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.clinimag.2026.110856 [doi]
AB  - Radiology reports are the primary product of diagnostic radiology, yet the 
      language in which they are written is rarely taught formally. The difference 
      between "no acute findings" and "normal study" can shape clinical decisions, 
      medicolegal exposure, and a trainee's emerging professional voice, but most 
      residents learn these distinctions by osmosis rather than instruction. We argue 
      that semantic precision in radiology reporting is a teachable skill and propose 
      five educational interventions to bring it out of the hidden curriculum: monthly 
      structured report-writing workshops, peer-review exercises with faculty feedback, 
      revised evaluation templates that explicitly score semantic clarity, integration 
      of natural language processing tools for real-time linguistic feedback and 
      dedicated didactic time treating language as a core competency. We draw on a 
      narrative literature review of language use in radiology, the hidden curriculum 
      in medical education, and natural language processing in reporting. We describe 
      how each intervention can be staged across the four-year residency and adapted to 
      subspecialty context, discuss how trainees can communicate calibrated confidence 
      rather than default to vague hedging, and consider the role of artificial 
      intelligence in supporting rather than replacing the radiologist's voice. 
      Formalizing semantic training signals that clear, calibrated language is as 
      clinically meaningful as accurate interpretation. Mastering radiology is not only 
      about interpreting images. It is about building a voice that speaks clearly, 
      confidently, and clinically.
