TI  - Rethinking the personal statement in the AI era.
PG  - 148-151
LID - 10.1093/acamed/wvaf038 [doi]
AB  - Personal statements for medical school and residency are often viewed as 
      authentic accounts of an applicant's fit and interest within a specific medical 
      context. Yet these narratives remain an inconsistent predictor of academic 
      performance, and generative artificial intelligence (AI) may undermine their 
      authenticity. Now is the time for admissions committees to convene stakeholders 
      and reassess the value and purpose of personal statements for both admissions and 
      applicants. While some may be tempted to do away with personal statements 
      entirely, these statements can provide meaningful opportunities for reflection 
      and growth. This Scholarly Perspective recommends that programs focus on the 
      humanity of the writing process by: (1) viewing the personal statement as a 
      learning opportunity for the applicant, (2) fostering community and collaboration 
      in the writing process, (3) crafting more specific prompts, and (4) intentionally 
      incorporating interview questions referencing the applicant's statement. By doing 
      so, personal statements can humanize the admissions process, even in an era of 
      AI.
