VI  - 74
IP  - 1
DP  - 2010 Jul
TI  - Informatic surgery: the union of surgeon and machine.
PG  - 118-20
LID - 10.1016/j.wneu.2010.04.005 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Surgical robotics present new and unique opportunities for the 
      training and practice of neurosurgery, beyond the promise of the minimally 
      invasive paradigm. METHODS: Robotic systems have been developed that simulate the 
      sight, sound, and touch of surgery allowing surgical training to evolve past an 
      apprenticeship and patient-based model towards standardization and virtual 
      training. RESULTS: The development of data-driven surgery, incorporating all 
      information available to the human senses and advanced imaging modalities, give 
      the modern surgeon an abundance of knowledge of the operative objectives and 
      surgical site. Notwithstanding the automation of computers, the surgeon must not 
      be excluded from this feedback loop as computer hardware and software is as-yet 
      unable to compare to human data synthesis and decision making. CONCLUSIONS: It is 
      this union of surgeon and machine and the continued evolution of surgery toward a 
      data-driven science rather than an experiential art that are required for the 
      definitive advancement of patient outcomes.
