TI  - Virtual reality simulation in neurosurgery: technologies and evolution.
PG  - 154-64
LID - 10.1227/NEU.0b013e3182750d26 [doi]
AB  - Neurosurgeons are faced with the challenge of learning, planning, and performing 
      increasingly complex surgical procedures in which there is little room for error. 
      With improvements in computational power and advances in visual and haptic 
      display technologies, virtual surgical environments can now offer potential 
      benefits for surgical training, planning, and rehearsal in a safe, simulated 
      setting. This article introduces the various classes of surgical simulators and 
      their respective purposes through a brief survey of representative simulation 
      systems in the context of neurosurgery. Many technical challenges currently limit 
      the application of virtual surgical environments. Although we cannot yet expect a 
      digital patient to be indistinguishable from reality, new developments in 
      computational methods and related technology bring us closer every day. We 
      recognize that the design and implementation of an immersive virtual reality 
      surgical simulator require expert knowledge from many disciplines. This article 
      highlights a selection of recent developments in research areas related to 
      virtual reality simulation, including anatomic modeling, computer graphics and 
      visualization, haptics, and physics simulation, and discusses their implication 
      for the simulation of neurosurgery.
