TI  - Generative AI use in instructor-assigned learning activities among nursing 
      students: A cross-sectional survey of perceptions, ethical awareness, faculty 
      restrictions, and continuance intention.
PG  - 107162
LID - S0260-6917(26)00190-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107162 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools are increasingly 
      embedded in nursing students' learning. Despite widespread adoption, it remains 
      unclear how nursing students' perceptions of GenAI, ethical awareness, and 
      perceived faculty restrictions relate to their intention to use GenAI in 
      instructor-assigned learning activities. AIM: This study examined nursing 
      students' purposes for using GenAI, perceptions of its use, ethical awareness, 
      perceived faculty restrictions, and factors associated with their intentions to 
      use GenAI in instructor-assigned learning activities. METHODOLOGY: A 
      cross-sectional survey design was employed to examine factors associated with 
      nursing students' intentions to use GenAI in instructor-assigned learning 
      activities. METHODS: Data were collected using an anonymous online questionnaire 
      administered to a convenience sample of 266 nursing students at a university in 
      northern Taiwan. The survey assessed patterns of GenAI use, perceptions of its 
      use, ethical awareness, perceived faculty restrictions, and intentions to use 
      GenAI. Descriptive statistics and hierarchical multiple regression analyses were 
      conducted to identify associated factors. RESULTS: ChatGPT was the most commonly 
      used GenAI tool. All respondents reported using it for professional inquiries, 
      academic writing, and summarizing long texts. Students demonstrated highly 
      favorable perceptions of GenAI, particularly regarding learning effectiveness and 
      time efficiency. Hierarchical multiple regression analyses revealed that 
      perceived learning enhancement, time savings, access to novel insights, 
      personalized feedback, and awareness of potential inaccuracies in GenAI responses 
      were significant predictors of intentions to use GenAI in instructor-assigned 
      learning activities. In contrast, ethical concerns and perceived faculty 
      restrictions were not significant predictors. CONCLUSION: Perceived academic 
      benefits were the strongest predictors of nursing students' intentions to use 
      GenAI in instructor-assigned learning activities, whereas ethical awareness and 
      perceived faculty restrictions were not significantly associated. Nursing 
      educators should intentionally integrate GenAI into instructional design and 
      learning tasks as a structured learning assistant, supported by clear guidance 
      and faculty oversight to promote responsible and effective use.
