TI  - Enhancing Clinical Reasoning in Surgical Education: A Randomized Crossover Trial 
      on AI-Generated Individual Feedback for Key Feature Question-Based Online 
      Assignments.
PG  - 104068
LID - S1931-7204(26)00200-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jsurg.2026.104068 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The positive effect of self-testing has been proven extensively, and 
      past research suggested that feedback could further enhance this effect. This 
      study compared the effectiveness of 2 types of feedback for student entries into 
      an app on learning outcomes regarding clinical reasoning in surgery (RQ1). 
      Additionally, we investigated the effect of general app usage (RQ2) and more 
      intensive app usage (RQ3). METHODS: Fourth-year medical students at a German 
      university participated in an entry exam and were then invited to participate in 
      the intervention, which consisted of a self-developed app sending daily 
      notifications with key feature questions throughout the semester, aligned to 
      lecture topics. In a crossover design, every student received either personalized 
      or general feedback. The learning outcome was measured in an exit exam. RESULTS: 
      There was no difference in exit exam score between the feedback conditions 
      (p = 0. 454). General app usage alone was not associated with a higher exit exam 
      score compared to nonusage, measured through a t-test (p = 0.067), but 
      explorative ANOVAs detected significant interactions between time and app use 
      (p = 0.032). Lastly, we found that higher intensity of app usage was associated 
      with significantly higher scores and therefore a larger learning benefit over 
      time, manifesting in a significant main effect for group (p = 0.003) and a 
      significant interaction effect (p < 0.001). CONCLUSIONS: Our results provide 
      further evidence for the testing effect and demonstrate the successful use of key 
      feature questions in surgical education. Since the results regarding the effect 
      of the feedback types are mixed and the relationship between the constructs seems 
      more complicated, further investigation is needed.
