TI  - Study on the Training Effect of SIMROID Robot in Improving Medical Students' 
      Patient-Clinician Communication and Behavior Management Skills.
PG  - 931-939
LID - 10.1002/jdd.70076 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: The aim of the study was to evaluate the effects of different SIMROID 
      robot training approaches in improving the patient-clinician communication 
      ability of dental students. METHODS: A total of 82 fourth-grade students from 
      Peking University Medical College of Stomatology were included in the study, and 
      divided into an experimental group (43 cases) and a control group (39 cases). In 
      the experimental group, students first practiced with SIMROID robots to simulate 
      patient training, followed by peer discussions. Training was conducted once a 
      week for 2 hours each time, for a total of 6 weeks. The control group used the 
      same SIMROID robot training, but with a reversed sequence-students first engaged 
      in peer communication followed by robot training. The study comprehensively 
      evaluates students' abilities in patient-clinician communication and behavior 
      management by means of Objective Structured Clinical Examination (OSCE), 
      operational process record analysis and observational scoring. Statistical 
      analysis uses SPSS 25.0 software to evaluate the differences in communication 
      skills, operational standardization and behavior management ability between the 
      two groups in different periods. RESULTS: In the OSCE assessment, the scores of 
      the experimental group in preoperative communication and patient comfort, 
      emergency management, and postoperative communication and feedback were 
      significantly higher than those of the control group (p < 0.05). The analysis of 
      operation process records showed that the experimental group showed progressive 
      improvement in operational performance across weeks, though weekly comparisons 
      did not reach statistical significance (p > 0.05). Among the observation scores, 
      the scores of the experimental group in the sterile operation specification, the 
      patient's safety consciousness, and the operation process continuity were always 
      higher than those of the control group (p < 0.05). The results of mentor 
      evaluation and student reflection indicated that the experimental group based on 
      initial SIMROID robot training followed by peer discussion was superior to the 
      control group in skills mastery, operational standardization, and strain 
      capacity. CONCLUSION: The teaching method based on initial SIMROID robot training 
      followed by peer discussion is superior to the reversed sequence approach in 
      improving the patient-clinician communication ability and emergency handling 
      ability, and has better teaching effect and practical application value.
