TI  - Using Graphic Medicine to Explore Perceptions of Professionalism in Early Medical 
      Education: A Pilot Study.
PG  - 586240
LID - 10.2147/AMEP.S586240 [doi]
LID - 586240
AB  - BACKGROUND: Lapses in professionalism remain a critical concern in medical 
      education, with early unprofessional behaviors linked to adverse outcomes in 
      clinical practice. Traditional interventions focus on reflection and mentorship, 
      but alternative approaches such as graphic medicine may provide new opportunities 
      for awareness and discussion. OBJECTIVE: This pilot study sought to evaluate 
      comics depicting professional and unprofessional behaviors as a tool for 
      measuring medical student awareness of professionalism, and assess perceptual 
      discrepancies between students and supervising faculty. METHODS: Faculty at the 
      University of Louisville School of Medicine provided examples of professional and 
      unprofessional student behaviors. These examples were transformed into comics 
      using ChatGPT, each depicting both classroom and clinical contexts. M2/M3 medical 
      students and supervising faculty were then asked to rate the professionalism 
      depicted in each comic using a Qualtrics-based survey and to provide qualitative 
      feedback on the graphic medicine approach. RESULTS: Faculty (N=14) and students 
      (N=20) differed in their perception of professionalism, with students rating 
      unprofessional behaviors as less extreme in comparison. Conversely, professional 
      behaviors were rated more positively by faculty than by students. While both 
      groups considered the comics realistic and engaging, participants questioned 
      their effectiveness as a learning tool in the survey format. Students also 
      completed the survey more quickly than faculty on average, suggesting less 
      engagement with the material in its existing survey format. CONCLUSION: 
      Discrepancies in perceptions of professionalism between students and faculty may 
      explain lapses in medical professionalism exhibited by M2/M3 students at the 
      University of Louisville. Though provided in survey format, graphic medicine may 
      serve as an engaging entry point for discussion, through integration into 
      small-group or reflective learning environments, for effective prevention of 
      medical professional decline.
