OAB - Lapses in professionalism remain a critical concern among medical students, with 
      early unprofessional behaviors linked to undesired outcomes in clinical practice. 
      This study sought to evaluate the use of comics as a way to measure medical 
      professional awareness among students and the gap in professional perception 
      between students and their supervising faculty. Specifically, students and 
      faculty were provided with comics depicting professional or unprofessional 
      behavior in the classroom that were considered to have clear translation to 
      clinical practice. Recipients of these comics were then asked to simply rank each 
      behavior on a numeric scale in an online survey platform, permitting statistical 
      evaluation of responses. Results pointed to a trending gap in perception between 
      students and faculty, with unprofessional behaviors deemed less extreme (toward 
      the middle of the numeric scale) by students than their supervising faculty. 
      While both groups deemed the comics realistic and engaging, participants 
      questioned their effectiveness as a learning tool in the existing survey format, 
      suggesting comics may serve as an effective prevention of medical professional 
      decline when presented in small-group discussion among the students and faculty.
